#{\footnote what_is}${\footnote What BASILISK 16 Is}+{\footnote main:010}K{\footnote BASILISK 16;Sound Recorder;Windows 3.11;emulator;machine;about;sound card}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b What BASILISK 16 Is\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 BASILISK 16 is a plugin instrument for a {\ul DAW}{\v term_daw}. It is a VST3 for Windows and macOS, and also an Audio Unit on macOS.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Each copy of the plugin runs its own Windows for Workgroups 3.11 in an emulator. In that Windows, sixteen copies of Sound Recorder (SOUNDREC.EXE) are open. The plugin calls each copy a {\ul recorder}{\v term_recorder}.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 When you play a note, the plugin chooses a recorder, and Windows presses its Play button. Sound Recorder plays its own sound through the emulated sound card, at the pitch of your note. The plugin sends that sound to your DAW.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The sound is a {\ul DNA}{\v term_dna}: a line of Sound Recorder commands. The plugin keeps the DNA in your project, so the project opens with the same sound.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b When the plugin loads\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The sixteen Sound Recorders lose their menus and title bars. They line up into one synthesizer, with Clock and a panel. This takes less than 6 seconds. Windows 3.11 is inside the plugin, so there is nothing to download.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b The sound card\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The Sound Blaster driver of Windows 3.11 plays one sound at a time. So the emulator has one more card, with 32 voices, and its own Windows 3.11 driver. With it, all sixteen recorders can play at the same time.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b See Also\par
\pard\sa35\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\uldb Playing the Instrument}{\v playing}\par
\pard\sa35\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\uldb The DNA}{\v dna}\par
\page
#{\footnote playing}${\footnote Playing the Instrument}+{\footnote main:020}K{\footnote playing;notes;MIDI;velocity;pitch bend;parameters;latency;automation;renders;CPU}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b Playing the Instrument\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Put BASILISK 16 on an instrument track in your DAW and play MIDI notes. You do not have to open the editor.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b DAW parameters\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Attack, Decay, Sustain, Release, Velocity, Bend Range, Output Gain and Transpose. You can automate all of them. Each one changes the globals of the {\ul DNA}{\v term_dna}. The parameter Current Program selects a factory preset.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Timing\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The plugin tells the DAW a fixed {\ul latency}{\v term_latency} of 60 ms (2880 samples at 48 kHz, with blocks of up to 512 samples). Larger blocks add their length. The DAW moves the sound back by that amount on playback. When you play live, you hear the delay.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Each note starts at an exact sample. Each instrument has sixteen recorders. When all of them play, a new note takes the oldest one. See {\ul voice stealing}{\v term_steal}.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b New sounds while you play\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 When the DNA changes, Windows rebuilds the recorders one at a time. The old sound keeps playing between them, so a preset change does not stop the music.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Offline renders\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 An offline render waits for each rebuild. A render, or a project that you open again, gives the same result each time, to the bit.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b To play one strip\par
\pard\sa60\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 1\tab Open the editor.\par
\pard\sa60\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 2\tab Click the Play button of a strip.\par
\pard\sa60\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 3\tab Click Stop to stop it.\par
\page
#{\footnote strips}${\footnote The Strips}+{\footnote main:030}K{\footnote strips;recorders;Play button;Stop button;wave display;position;length;seek bar;Clock}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b The Strips\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Each strip is one real Sound Recorder, without its menu bar and title bar. There are sixteen strips. The headings above them are:\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Status\tab Stopped or Playing.\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Wave\tab The green wave display. It follows the sound.\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Position\tab The time of the play position, in seconds.\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Length\tab The length of the sound, in seconds.\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Seek\tab The scroll bar for the position.\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Rewind\tab Moves to the start.\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Forward\tab Moves to the end.\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Play\tab Plays the sound of this recorder.\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Stop\tab Stops this recorder.\par
\pard\sa60\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Record\tab Sound Recorder's own Record button. The instrument does not use it.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A note can play more than one recorder. Harmony, unison and layers all add recorders to one note. See {\uldb Voices and Layers}{\v voices}.\par
\page
#{\footnote presets}${\footnote Presets}+{\footnote main:040}K{\footnote presets;factory presets;programs;saving sounds}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b Presets\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 BASILISK 16 has 16 factory presets. They are in the preset list of your DAW. Each preset is a {\ul DNA}{\v term_dna}.\par
\pard\sa35\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20\b Name\tab DNA\par
\pard\sa35\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Ding Piano\tab {\f1 ding#16;R250}\par
\pard\sa35\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Chime Octaves\tab {\f1 chimesH0,12;R500}\par
\pard\sa35\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Telephone Choir\tab {\f1 ringinU3~12;A60S90R700}\par
\pard\sa35\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Reverse Ding Swell\tab {\f1 dingR;A10R300}\par
\pard\sa35\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Echo Bells\tab {\f1 chimesE+;R900}\par
\pard\sa35\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Slow Ring Pad\tab {\f1 ringout<<E;A400D300S80R1500}\par
\pard\sa35\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Fifth Stack\tab {\f1 dingH0,7,12;R300}\par
\pard\sa35\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Major Ding\tab {\f1 dingH0,4,7;R250}\par
\pard\sa35\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Ding and Chimes\tab {\f1 ding#8/chimes#8T12;R350}\par
\pard\sa35\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Chopped Ring\tab {\f1 ringin@50]>;R120}\par
\pard\sa35\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Insert and Mix\tab {\f1 dingIchimesMringin;R300}\par
\pard\sa35\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Triple Echo Reverse\tab {\f1 chimesEEER-;A20R1000}\par
\pard\sa35\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Bass Ding\tab {\f1 ding<T-12;R250}\par
\pard\sa35\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Detuned Unison\tab {\f1 dingU4~25;R300}\par
\pard\sa35\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Ring Out Octaves\tab {\f1 ringout>H0,12,24;R160}\par
\pard\sa60\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Copy Paste Stutter\tab {\f1 ding@30]CPPP;R160}\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Your own presets\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Save a preset with the preset menu of your DAW. The preset keeps the DNA. A preset from BASILISK SYNTH, the Windows XP edition, also plays here; sounds that Windows 3.11 does not have play DING.WAV.\par
