#{\footnote dna}${\footnote The DNA}+{\footnote main:050}K{\footnote DNA;recipe;DNA line;Undo;Clear;sound, changing}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b The DNA\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The whole sound of the instrument is one line of text, the DNA. Windows builds the sound from it with the real menu commands of Sound Recorder, in order from left to right. Because the order changes the result, there are very many different sounds.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Example:\par
\pard\sa60\li395\ri175\plain\f1\fs20 chimesE+;R900\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 chimes}\tab Load CHIMES.WAV.\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 E}\tab Add Echo.\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 +}\tab Increase Volume.\par
\pard\sa60\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 ;R900}\tab Globals: release over 900 ms. See {\ul envelope}{\v term_env}.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A slash ({\f1 /}) starts another {\ul layer}{\v term_layer}: {\f1 ding#8/chimes#8T12} is DING.WAV on 8 recorders and CHIMES.WAV an octave up on the other 8.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b To change the DNA\par
\pard\sa60\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 1\tab Open the editor.\par
\pard\sa60\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 2\tab Type in the DNA line and press ENTER. ESC puts the line back as it was.\par
\pard\sa60\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \tab Or use the buttons and sliders of the panel. See {\uldb The Panel}{\v menus}.\par
\pard\sa60\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 3\tab Windows rebuilds the recorders. The DAW gets the new DNA and saves it with your project.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Rules\par
\pard\sa35\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab A DNA has no spaces and is at most 1024 characters.\par
\pard\sa35\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Up to 8 layers, and up to 64 steps in each layer.\par
\pard\sa35\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab A step that makes a sound longer than 30 seconds is skipped.\par
\pard\sa60\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Unknown characters are skipped. A number out of range is set to the nearest limit.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b See Also\par
\pard\sa35\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\uldb DNA Reference}{\v dna_ref}\par
\pard\sa35\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\uldb Effects and Edits}{\v effects}\par
\page
#{\footnote voices}${\footnote Voices and Layers}+{\footnote main:060}K{\footnote voices;layers;harmony;unison;transpose;level;recorders, number of;pool}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b Voices and Layers\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Layers\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A {\ul layer}{\v term_layer} is one part of the sound. It has a source sound, its steps and its voice genes. Each note plays all layers at the same time. Each layer has its own recorders, its {\ul pool}{\v term_pool}.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 On the panel, the < and > buttons select a layer ("Layer 1 of 2"). The buttons and sliders change the selected layer.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Voices\par
\pard\sa60\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Voices\tab Recorders for this layer, 1 to 16 ({\f1 #}).\par
\pard\sa60\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Transpose\tab Semitones up or down ({\f1 T}).\par
\pard\sa60\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Unison\tab 1 to 8 voices, a little out of tune ({\f1 U}). See {\ul unison}{\v term_unison}.\par
\pard\sa60\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Level\tab Layer level, 0 to 400% ({\f1 L}).\par
\pard\sa60\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Harmony\tab Click to step through Single, Octave, Fifth, Major, Minor and Octaves ({\f1 H}). See {\ul harmony}{\v term_harmony}.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b Note:} Harmony and unison use more recorders for each note. With many voices, notes take recorders from each other sooner.\par
\page
#{\footnote effects}${\footnote Effects and Edits}+{\footnote main:070}K{\footnote effects;echo;reverse;speed;volume;Insert File;Mix with File;Copy;Paste;Delete Before;Delete After;position}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b Effects and Edits\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 These are the real commands of Sound Recorder. Each Effects button on the panel adds a step to the DNA of the selected layer. Windows then runs the steps in Sound Recorder, in order.\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20\b Button\tab Command\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Louder\tab Increase Volume ({\f1 +})\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Softer\tab Decrease Volume ({\f1 -})\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Faster\tab Increase Speed ({\f1 >}): half the length, an octave up\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Slower\tab Decrease Speed ({\f1 <}): two times the length, an octave down\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Echo\tab Add Echo ({\f1 E})\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Reverse\tab Reverse ({\f1 R})\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Del <\tab Delete Before Current Position ({\f1 [})\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Del >\tab Delete After Current Position ({\f1 ]})\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Copy\tab Copy the whole sound of the layer ({\f1 C})\par
\pard\sa60\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Paste\tab Paste Insert at the position ({\f1 P})\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Type these steps in the DNA line:\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 X}\tab Paste Mix at the position\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 I}{\f1\i name}\tab Insert File: insert another sound at the position\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 M}{\f1\i name}\tab Mix with File: mix another sound in at the position\par
\pard\sa60\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 @}{\f1\i NN}\tab Move the position to NN% of the length\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The position starts at 0 when the source sound loads. Each step leaves it where Sound Recorder leaves it. Example: {\f1 ringin@50]>} keeps the first half of RINGIN.WAV and plays it an octave up.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b Clear} removes all steps of the selected layer. {\b Undo} goes back to the DNA before the last change.\par
\page
#{\footnote sounds}${\footnote The Sounds}+{\footnote main:080}K{\footnote sounds;source sounds;CHIMES.WAV;DING.WAV;RINGIN.WAV;RINGOUT.WAV;names of sounds}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b The Sounds\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Windows for Workgroups 3.11 has four sounds in {\f1 C:\\WINDOWS}. The Sound buttons on the panel make one of them the source of the selected layer.\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 chimes}\tab CHIMES.WAV\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 ding}\tab DING.WAV\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 ringin}\tab RINGIN.WAV\par
\pard\sa60\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 ringout}\tab RINGOUT.WAV\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The other sound names of the DNA, also {\f1 chord} and {\f1 tada}, play DING.WAV here. The Windows XP edition has 35 sounds.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The note that plays a sound at its own rate is the {\ul root note}{\v term_root}, middle C (60) unless the DNA sets another one.\par
\page
#{\footnote minimize}${\footnote Minimizing the Synth to Use Windows}+{\footnote main:090}K{\footnote minimize;restore;icon;Program Manager;Windows 3.11, using;help, opening;F1}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b Minimizing the Synth to Use Windows\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The synth window covers the screen of Windows 3.11. You can minimize it and use Windows as usual: Program Manager, File Manager, Notepad, Paintbrush or this Help. The instrument keeps playing while it is minimized.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b To minimize the synth\par
\pard\sa60\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Click the Minimize button at the right end of the synth's title bar.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The synth becomes the BASILISK 16 icon at the bottom of the screen. Program Manager is an icon there too.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b To restore the synth\par
\pard\sa60\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Double-click the BASILISK 16 icon.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b To open this Help\par
\pard\sa35\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab In the synth, press F1.\par
\pard\sa60\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Double-click the Program Manager icon. In the BASILISK 16 group, double-click {\b BASILISK 16 Help}.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b Note:} The system sounds of this Windows are off, so they do not play into your music. When you remove the plugin from your project, this Windows stops. Changes to its files are not kept.\par
\page
#{\footnote menus}${\footnote The Panel}+{\footnote main:100}K{\footnote panel;DNA line;Sound buttons;Effects buttons;sliders;Undo;Clear;Harmony}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b The Panel\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The panel is below the strips. Each change rebuilds the recorders and becomes the sound of the plugin.\par
\pard\sa60\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 DNA\tab The DNA line. ENTER applies it; ESC puts it back.\par
\pard\sa60\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Sound\tab chimes, ding, ringin, ringout: the source of the selected layer.\par
\pard\sa60\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Effects\tab Sound Recorder's commands. See {\uldb Effects and Edits}{\v effects}.\par
\pard\sa60\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 <  >\tab Select the previous or next layer.\par
\pard\sa60\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Undo\tab Go back to the DNA before the last change.\par
\pard\sa60\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Clear\tab Remove the steps of the selected layer.\par
\pard\sa60\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Harmony\tab Step through the harmony choices.\par
\pard\sa60\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Sliders\tab Voices, Transpose, Unison and Level for the selected layer. Attack, Decay, Sustain and Release for the whole sound. See {\ul envelope}{\v term_env}.\par
\page
#{\footnote dna_ref}${\footnote DNA Reference}+{\footnote main:110}K{\footnote DNA;reference;steps;genes;globals;attack;decay;sustain;release;velocity;bend range;root note;gain;fixed pitch}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b DNA Reference\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A DNA is: layer {\f1 /} layer {\f1 /} ... {\f1 ;} globals. Each layer is a sound name, then steps and voice genes in any order.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Steps (run in order)\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 +  -}\tab Increase / Decrease Volume\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 >  <}\tab Increase / Decrease Speed\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 E}\tab Add Echo\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 R}\tab Reverse\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 @}{\f1\i NN}\tab Move the position to NN% (0 to 99)\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 [  ]}\tab Delete Before / After Current Position\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 C  P  X}\tab Copy, Paste Insert, Paste Mix\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 I}{\f1\i name}\tab Insert File\par
\pard\sa60\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 M}{\f1\i name}\tab Mix with File\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Voice genes (the last one of each kind wins)\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 #}{\f1\i N}\tab Recorders for this layer, 1 to 16\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 T}{\f1\i N}\tab Transpose in semitones, -48 to +48\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 H}{\f1\i a,b,...}\tab Harmony intervals in semitones, up to 8\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 U}{\f1\i N}{\f1 ~}{\f1\i C}\tab Unison: N voices (1 to 8) over +/- C cents (0 to 100)\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 F}\tab Fixed pitch: always the file's own rate\par
\pard\sa60\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 L}{\f1\i N}\tab Layer level in %, 0 to 400\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Globals (after the semicolon)\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 A}{\f1\i N}\tab Attack in ms (default 0)\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 D}{\f1\i N}\tab Decay in ms (default 0)\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 S}{\f1\i N}\tab Sustain level in % (default 100)\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 R}{\f1\i N}\tab Release in ms (default 120)\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 B}{\f1\i N}\tab Pitch-bend range in semitones (default 2)\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 V}{\f1\i N}\tab Velocity sensitivity in % (default 100)\par
\pard\sa35\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 K}{\f1\i N}\tab Root MIDI note (default 60)\par
\pard\sa60\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 G}{\f1\i N}\tab Output gain in dB (default 0)\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Recorders for each layer\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Each layer gets at least one recorder. Layers with {\f1 #} get their number first, in order. Layers without {\f1 #} share the rest equally.\par
\page
#{\footnote trouble}${\footnote Troubleshooting}+{\footnote main:120}K{\footnote troubleshooting;problems;no sound;silence;late notes;CPU;macOS, quarantine;DAW does not show the plugin}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b Troubleshooting\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b The DAW does not show the plugin\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Windows: copy the {\f1 Basilisk16.vst3} folder to {\f1 C:\\Program Files\\Common Files\\VST3}, then rescan plugins. macOS: the plugin is not notarized. Run this once in Terminal, then restart the DAW:\par
\pard\sa60\li395\ri175\plain\f1\fs20 xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine ~/Library/Audio/Plug-Ins/VST3/Basilisk16.vst3 ~/Library/Audio/Plug-Ins/Components/Basilisk16.component\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b There is no sound\par
\pard\sa35\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Check that the track gets MIDI notes.\par
\pard\sa60\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Open the editor. If the strips do not move when you play, remove the plugin and add it again.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Notes sound late when I play live\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 This is the fixed {\ul latency}{\v term_latency} of 60 ms. Recorded MIDI plays on time, because the DAW compensates.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Some notes are cut short\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 All sixteen recorders were busy, so a new note took one. Use fewer harmony or unison voices, or a shorter release.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b The computer gets busy\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Each instance is an emulated PC. On the PC it was built on, it uses about 13% of one core when idle and about 35% while it plays.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b I minimized the synth and cannot find it\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Double-click the BASILISK 16 icon at the bottom of the screen. See {\uldb Minimizing the Synth to Use Windows}{\v minimize}.\par
\page
#{\footnote coming}${\footnote Coming Next}+{\footnote main:130}K{\footnote coming next;DNA version 2;offsets;pan;envelopes;macros;library}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b Coming Next\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The DNA format has a version 2. Its specification and the shared DNA code are written; the instruments do not use it yet. Version 2 only adds. Each DNA that works now keeps its meaning.\par
\pard\sa35\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab A start offset for each layer, so layers can start one after another.\par
\pard\sa35\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Pan for each layer, from left to right.\par
\pard\sa35\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Drawn volume and pan envelopes, with up to 8 points.\par
\pard\sa35\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Positions in milliseconds, for collages of several sounds.\par
\pard\sa35\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Four macros for each sound, which the DAW can automate.\par
\pard\sa60\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab A library disc with more Windows sounds and presets.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_dna}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b DNA}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 One line of text that is the whole sound: layers of Windows sounds, each changed by Sound Recorder commands in order, and the settings for voices and the envelope.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_recorder}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b recorder}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 One of the sixteen Sound Recorders in Windows 3.11. Each recorder holds one sound and plays one voice at a time.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_layer}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b layer}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 One part of the sound, with its own source sound, steps and recorders. Each note plays all layers.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_pool}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b pool}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The recorders that belong to one layer. Set the number with {\f1 #} or with the Voices slider.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_harmony}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b harmony}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Intervals in semitones that each note plays together. {\f1 H0,4,7} plays a major triad. {\f1 H0,12,-12} plays the note and an octave on each side.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_unison}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b unison}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 More voices of the same note, each a few cents out of tune, for a wide sound. {\f1 U3~12} plays 3 voices over 12 cents up and down.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_env}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b envelope}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 How the volume of a note changes: Attack (time to full volume), Decay (time to the sustain level), Sustain (the level while you hold the key) and Release (time to silence after you let go). The emulated sound card applies it to each voice.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_latency}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b latency}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The fixed delay between a note and its sound. The plugin tells the DAW the delay, and the DAW moves recorded parts back to the beat.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_steal}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b voice stealing}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 When all recorders of a layer play, a new note stops the oldest one, with a 5 ms fade, and uses it.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_daw}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b DAW}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A digital audio workstation: the music program that loads the plugin, for example Ableton Live, Bitwig, Cubase, FL Studio, Logic Pro or REAPER.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_root}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b root note}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The MIDI note that plays a sound at the rate it was recorded. Set it with {\f1 K} in the globals. The default is 60, middle C.\par
\page
}
